And by the way the emacs manual is a spiral bound
document maybe 200 pages long and available for sale
from the Free Software Foundaton (it's a big part of
how they support their efforts). When you hear that
emacs is "big", we mean "big". The manual, a copy of
which I once owned before many of life's more
colorful misadventures, is also very clear and
delightful to read even if you don't have or want to
use the editor.  It is what Nutshell Handbooks try
to be and sometimes are.

GNU's preferred online documentation is in "info"
files, and they frankly admit that the man pages are
being let go stale on purpose. Those pages are too
hard to create and to maintain.

What I use within Cygwin to learn emacs and look up
stuff is the command "info emacs". Navigating the
tree structured info documentation has a small
learning curve, but isn't really too challenging.
"info info" should get you started if setup
installed "info" at all. Emacs also has context
sensitive help available via <ctrl-h> (maddeningly
why the backspace functionality starts out set to
DEL instead of to the backspace key, or at least
did the last time I used emacs).



